 pressure results in the  formation of shock waves  in the transonic range of speeds the  buffeting of the aircraft  results from alternate collapse and  re-establishment of flow due to an  unstable shockwave formation  it's been reported that the buffeting  may cause a momentary blurring of the  pilot's vision  when the aircraft passes through this  speed range of mixed airflows  it has passed through the sonic barrier  at supersonic speeds the shock waves  become more stable  and form an increasingly acute angle as  speed increases  the air in shock waves is optically more  dense than normal air  this density produces a deviation of  light rays which apparently causes a  displacement of objects from their true  positions  however this displacement is slight  there has been much discussion about  vibration of jet aircraft and  rocket-propelled aircraft in the  supersonic speed range  however no vibrations of intensities  great enough to harm  human eyes or seriously interfere with  visual performance have been reported  acceleration does not seriously  interfere with the vision of an  aviator who is wearing either a full  pressure suit or a good g  suit however without such protection an  acceleration of six g's will cause  practically any aviator who is in an  upright position to black out  naturally this effect is limiting on  high-speed flight  we now examine some of the factors that  relate to the pilot's recognition of  objects in the air  these factors are greatly affected by  high speeds and high altitudes  the size of the target is extremely  important in relation to whether it can  be  seen if the target moves away its size  decreases  if the overall illumination and contrast  between the target and its background  remain constant  there is a size below which the target  cannot be seen  we will use this triangle to demonstrate  size  under optimum conditions of illumination  in order to see and identify an object  five miles away  the object must be thirty feet in  diameter  some modern aircraft are less than the  size when measured from a head-on  profile  as distance decreases the target size  increases until it reaches the size at  which it can be seen  another characteristic related to  recognition is the size of the visual  field in which the target must fall in  order to be seen  in studying this problem we will use  this drawing of the human eye  seen in cross section at maximum  distance  the target must fall on the exact center  of the retina  the area of best visual acuity in order  for the pilot to see it  this means the target must appear in a  small cone  less than one degree in cross-section  with the cone reaching the fovea  the exact center of the retina a target  twice the size required for central  vision can be recognized in a 10 degree  cone  a target four times the size required  for central vision  can be recognized anywhere in a 26  degree cone  the size of the cone in which a target  can be recognized  is also important because of the expanse  of sky a pilot must search  to detect or see a target a pilot has no  accurate way of knowing whether his  scanning pattern is fine enough  whether he is covering all areas of the  sky in which he can detect and recognize  the target  for example if this aircraft can be seen  only by foveal vision  this scan will miss it entirely  in addition at high altitudes the sky is  generally empty of any visual references  or cues that can serve as guides for a  systematic scanning pattern  if a target is seen and the eyes are  momentarily shifted away from it  there is difficulty in relocating it  because there are no visual cues  the boundaries and structure of the  windscreen do however provide a little  help as a visual reference  but when the pilot knows a target is  nearby  he often does not see it for example  at high altitudes and high speeds pilots  who are trying to rendezvous at a  pre-arranged position  have difficulty locating each other  pilots have chased other aircraft for  more than a hundred miles  knowing from their electronic gear that  they were always as near as five miles  and sometimes less than one mile away  and yet  were never able to sight the other  aircraft visually  these failures were not due to  navigational errors  but to a fundamental visual cause above  forty thousand feet  ninety percent of visual sightings made  by pilots  are by means of glints of light flashes  from the other aircraft  these glints occur only when the other  aircraft maneuvers so that reflections  from its surface happen to be toward the  direction of the pilot  in other words at high altitudes and  high speeds  if the other aircraft does not glint it  probably will not be seen  another reason for failure to see other  aircraft at high altitudes  is that the pilot having nothing distant  to look at  unconsciously keeps his eyes in focus  for objects nearby  for practical purposes he is blind to  objects in space  he may even fail to see other aircraft  that are not far away  to avoid this he should try to focus his  eyes at least 20 feet away which is the  same as for infinity  in recognition and closure at high  speeds the most limiting factor with  which the pilot must contend  is his own lag and visual perception and  the time he requires to react to the  sight of the object  specifically this is the time it takes a  pilot to see  identify and act the instant an object  enters his field of vision and  immediately thereafter  there are certain physiological tasks to  be performed  by the pilot's eyes brain and muscles  each of these tasks take some time in  some instances it takes  only a very short interval but an  interval in which a high-speed aircraft  may travel hundreds or even thousands of  feet  we will examine a routine problem a  pilot  represented by this eye is flying at 600  knots  when an object enters his peripheral  vision that is just at the corner of his  eye  before showing the problem in motion we  will use this 10 second stopwatch  and we'll analyze what is to happen in  terms of time  it will take one tenth of a second for  the sensation of the object to reach the  brain  about three-tenths of a second will pass  while the brain  signals the eye to move and the lens  focuses the object on the phobia  nearly seven tenths of a second will  pass before the brain recognizes the  object as an aircraft  two seconds will pass while the brain  decides what to do  and signals the muscles nearly two and a  half seconds will pass while the muscles  operate the controls and the aircraft  changes its flight path  this is a total of about five and a half  seconds  not a long time under normal  circumstances  but look at it again now in slow motion  with this object motionless and this  aircraft moving at 600 knots  the pilot will travel a hundred feet  before he even sees the object  he will travel nearly 400 feet before  his phobia picks up the object  he will travel over a thousand feet  before he recognizes the object as an  aircraft  he will travel a half mile before he can  decide what to do  and he will travel over four-fifths of a  mile  before his flight path changes  but of course both aircraft will be  moving at 600 knots  two pilots on opposite courses would  have to see each other at a distance of  11 000 feet  in order to change their flight pads in  time to avoid collision  at eighteen hundred knots these  distances will be trebled  the pilot will travel over a mile and a  half before he can decide what to do  and he will travel over two and a half  miles before he can alter his flight  path  imagine two aircraft heading for each  other at speeds of 1800 knots  if they emerge from cloud cover 600 feet  apart  neither pilot will see the other  aircraft before they collide  they emerge three miles apart they will  collide before the pilots can decide  what to do  and if they emerge five miles apart they  are still  too close to change their flight pads  some of the time intervals used in this  discussion are not subject to very  careful and accurate measurement  however if they are wrong it is probably  on the side of being too short  except for the response time of the  aircraft itself these time intervals are  due solely to the unchanging and  unalterable characteristics of the  normal human eye  brain and muscles the time intervals for  recognition and action  are based on conditions that are ideal  for example a clear day  an alert experienced pilot with no  distractions  and where only the simplest of tasks are  involved  these time intervals and distances could  increase by several seconds and  thousands of feet  due to any number of complications that  could occur in perfectly normal flight  for example anything that interfered  with the pilot's vision  such as a structural member of the  aircraft  haze or gray out induced by high  acceleration  any of these things could add to the  time the pilot requires to see  and recognize an object in the sky  additional problems of time and distance  arise when a pilot performs a relatively  quick and simple task  such as glancing down at a flight  instrument by the time he adjusts his  eyes to focus on the instrument  reads it then shifts his eyes to the sky  again  he will have traveled thousands of feet  while flying blind  this can have serious consequences for  example the pilot traveling at several  hundred knots  glances at his altimeter another  aircraft coming out of the clouds a mile  away  will be on top of him before he even  knows it's there  the reason is simple to look at the  instrument and readjust the eyes to the  interior illumination  takes about eight tenths of a second to  recognize the instrument reading  takes another eight tenths of a second  to look up and readjust to the outside  distant focus  takes another eight tenths of a second a  total of nearly two and a half seconds  during this time an aircraft at 600  knots  will travel 2400 feet while at 1800  knots it will travel about 7 200 feet  well over a mile  the time interval could be greatly  increased where the pilot looks from a  bright sky to a dimly lit panel  he must adapt to the dim light then he  must readjust to the brightness outside  even the short time consumed in reading  instruments  in seeing and identifying objects and in  reacting properly can be vital  in avoiding collisions and intercepting  other aircraft  nearly three-quarters of recent mid-air  collisions occurred in straight and  level flight  nearly all were attributed to failure of  a pilot to see the other aircraft in  time  in some cases the pilot never saw the  other aircraft at all  when the other aircraft was seen the  distance and rate of closure probably  were misjudged  or both pilots in attempting to turn  away turned into each other  many near-misses have been reported the  majority occurred on route during full  daylight with visibility to 15 miles or  more  near misses of today may be collisions  tomorrow when more  aircraft are traveling and at greater  speeds  to avoid a collision it is important  only that the other aircraft be seen in  time to keep from hitting it  this includes seeing it well enough to  establish its course  where the basic problem is preventing  collisions aircraft can be designed to  be as conspicuous as possible  this includes the use of paints and  markings as well as conspicuous lights  interception involves approaching other  aircraft deliberately  while avoiding collisions with them  interception includes rendezvousing with  friendly aircraft  it also includes identifying unreported  aircraft  and it includes attacking enemy aircraft  most of the time electronic equipment  both airborne and on the ground  helps a pilot to perform a mission of  interception however a pilot usually  must  actually see the aircraft he is  intercepting  as early as possible  he must see it at a great distance so he  can identify it positively  this frequently 